CASE = [
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> The Criminal Tribes Act of 1871 labelled certain communities as criminals by birth, and the Vernacular Press Act of 1878 restricted the Indian-language press. Elsewhere, apartheid in South Africa and Jim Crow laws in the United States separated people on grounds of race. (i) Which colonial Act labelled communities as criminals by birth? (ii) What did the Vernacular Press Act restrict? (iii) In which country was apartheid practised? (iv) Why is it not enough for an action to be 'legal' for it to be just?",
   "(i) Criminal Tribes Act of 1871 (1). (ii) Freedom of the Indian-language press (1). (iii) South Africa (1). (iv) Laws can themselves be unfair or discriminatory; justice also requires fairness and equality, which courts protect (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> India has a single hierarchy of courts: the Supreme Court in New Delhi at the top, 25 High Courts, and subordinate district courts below them. The Supreme Court can directly hear disputes between the Centre and a state, and the President can seek its opinion on a legal question. (i) Which is the apex court of India? (ii) How many High Courts does India have? (iii) What are the courts below the High Court called? (iv) Name the two jurisdictions of the Supreme Court mentioned in the passage.",
   "(i) Supreme Court (1). (ii) 25 (1). (iii) Subordinate courts (1). (iv) Original jurisdiction (Centre-state disputes) and advisory jurisdiction (President's reference under Article 143) (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> In the first reported Public Interest Litigation (PIL), lawyer Kapila Hingorani petitioned for undertrial prisoners in Bihar who had spent years in jail without trial. In 1997 the Vishaka guidelines on sexual harassment at the workplace came from another PIL. (i) What does PIL stand for? (ii) Who filed the first reported PIL? (iii) What do the Vishaka guidelines concern? (iv) How does PIL help the poor to get justice?",
   "(i) Public Interest Litigation (1). (ii) Kapila Hingorani (1). (iii) Sexual harassment at the workplace (1). (iv) Any person or group can approach the court for those who cannot afford it, so even disadvantaged people get justice (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

